1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,538
You open a map.

2
00:00:02,708 --> 00:00:04,246
There's the blue dot.

3
00:00:04,417 --> 00:00:05,186
That's you.

4
00:00:05,356 --> 00:00:08,553
Found in seconds, to
within about sixteen feet.

5
00:00:08,723 --> 00:00:11,800
You probably think your
phone is just smart.

6
00:00:11,970 --> 00:00:12,355
Nope.

7
00:00:12,525 --> 00:00:14,833
Your phone is listening to clocks.

8
00:00:15,002 --> 00:00:16,156
Clocks in space.

9
00:00:16,326 --> 00:00:22,215
And every one of those clocks would be
wrong, if nobody had listened to Einstein.

10
00:00:22,385 --> 00:00:28,154
A guy who died before the first satellite
ever launched is keeping your map honest.

11
00:00:28,325 --> 00:00:29,479
Let's see how.

12
00:00:31,598 --> 00:00:34,290
You already know the trick GPS uses.

13
00:00:34,461 --> 00:00:34,975
Thunderstorm.

14
00:00:35,015 --> 00:00:35,400
Flash.

15
00:00:35,570 --> 00:00:36,724
You start counting.

16
00:00:36,894 --> 00:00:39,187
One Mississippi, two Mississippi, three...

17
00:00:39,227 --> 00:00:39,612
Boom.

18
00:00:39,782 --> 00:00:42,474
Every five seconds is about a mile.

19
00:00:42,644 --> 00:00:48,533
The sound came late, and the delay told
you how far away the lightning was.

20
00:00:50,653 --> 00:00:55,004
GPS does the same thing, only
with light instead of sound.

21
00:00:55,174 --> 00:01:00,737
Every satellite keeps shouting two things:
exactly what time it is, and exactly

22
00:01:00,777 --> 00:01:01,648
where it is.

23
00:01:01,818 --> 00:01:06,818
That message takes less than a
tenth of a second to reach you.

24
00:01:06,988 --> 00:01:09,680
Your phone checks how late it arrived.

25
00:01:09,850 --> 00:01:11,773
The delay is your distance.

26
00:01:13,893 --> 00:01:18,628
Hear one satellite, and you're
somewhere on a giant ball around it.

27
00:01:18,798 --> 00:01:22,764
Hear three, and the balls
cross in just two spots.

28
00:01:22,935 --> 00:01:25,243
One is way out in space.

29
00:01:25,412 --> 00:01:27,335
The other one is you.

30
00:01:27,505 --> 00:01:33,250
A fourth one fixes your phone's own
clock, which, let's be honest, is cheap.

31
00:01:35,370 --> 00:01:36,524
Here's the catch.

32
00:01:36,694 --> 00:01:37,848
Light is fast.

33
00:01:38,018 --> 00:01:42,369
In one billionth of a second,
it goes about one foot.

34
00:01:42,538 --> 00:01:49,134
So if a satellite's clock is off by just
one millionth of a second, your distance

35
00:01:49,174 --> 00:01:51,889
to it is off by a thousand feet.

36
00:01:52,059 --> 00:01:58,082
That's why every GPS satellite carries
atomic clocks, good to a few billionths of a

37
00:01:58,122 --> 00:01:59,102
second a day.

38
00:01:59,272 --> 00:02:05,097
Simple plan: put perfect clocks in the
sky, sync them with the ground, done.

39
00:02:05,267 --> 00:02:08,729
A second up there is a second down here.

40
00:02:08,898 --> 00:02:09,283
Right?

41
00:02:09,453 --> 00:02:09,838
Wrong.

42
00:02:10,008 --> 00:02:10,393
Twice.

43
00:02:12,512 --> 00:02:14,250
Wrong number one: speed.

44
00:02:14,421 --> 00:02:19,817
GPS satellites race around the Earth at
about eight thousand seven hundred miles an

45
00:02:19,857 --> 00:02:20,190
hour.

46
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:23,052
Twice a day around the whole planet.

47
00:02:25,172 --> 00:02:27,864
Let's build the simplest clock there is.

48
00:02:28,035 --> 00:02:31,617
Two mirrors, one flash of
light bouncing between them.

49
00:02:31,786 --> 00:02:33,260
Up, down: tick.

50
00:02:33,430 --> 00:02:34,904
Up, down: tock.

51
00:02:35,674 --> 00:02:38,366
Now put that clock on a satellite.

52
00:02:38,536 --> 00:02:43,271
From down here, the light doesn't
go straight up and down anymore.

53
00:02:43,442 --> 00:02:48,682
The mirrors move while it flies, so
it takes a longer, slanted path.

54
00:02:49,852 --> 00:02:55,337
And here's what Einstein figured out:
the speed of light doesn't care how fast

55
00:02:55,377 --> 00:02:56,206
you're going.

56
00:02:56,375 --> 00:02:58,033
Same speed, longer path.

57
00:02:58,204 --> 00:03:00,127
So every tick takes longer.

58
00:03:00,297 --> 00:03:02,220
The moving clock runs slow.

59
00:03:02,390 --> 00:03:03,544
It's not broken.

60
00:03:03,714 --> 00:03:05,637
That's just what time does.

61
00:03:05,807 --> 00:03:11,312
For a GPS satellite, it's about seven
millionths of a second lost every day.

62
00:03:13,432 --> 00:03:15,740
Wrong number two is even weirder.

63
00:03:15,909 --> 00:03:17,447
Gravity slows time down.

64
00:03:17,618 --> 00:03:23,547
The closer you are to something big and
heavy, like the Earth, the slower your

65
00:03:23,587 --> 00:03:24,396
clock runs.

66
00:03:24,566 --> 00:03:26,994
Go higher, and time speeds up.

67
00:03:27,164 --> 00:03:30,241
You don't have to take that on faith.

68
00:03:30,411 --> 00:03:35,531
In 2010, scientists in Colorado raised
one super-precise clock by about a foot.

69
00:03:35,701 --> 00:03:37,624
The raised clock ran faster.

70
00:03:39,744 --> 00:03:43,710
So yes, your head is aging
faster than your feet.

71
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:50,087
We did the math: stand for eighty years,
and your head ends up almost half a

72
00:03:50,127 --> 00:03:52,277
millionth of a second older.

73
00:03:52,447 --> 00:03:55,139
The cat's been lying flat all day.

74
00:03:55,309 --> 00:03:56,847
Now you know why.

75
00:03:58,968 --> 00:04:01,276
GPS satellites aren't a foot up.

76
00:04:01,445 --> 00:04:06,180
They're over twelve thousand miles up,
where Earth's pull is much weaker.

77
00:04:06,351 --> 00:04:12,056
So their clocks run fast: about forty-five
millionths of a second ahead, every day.

78
00:04:14,175 --> 00:04:15,329
Put both together.

79
00:04:15,499 --> 00:04:16,853
Speed: minus seven.

80
00:04:17,023 --> 00:04:18,435
Height: plus forty-five.

81
00:04:18,547 --> 00:04:23,615
Every day, a satellite clock gets
ahead of clocks on the ground by about

82
00:04:23,655 --> 00:04:25,975
thirty-eight millionths of a second.

83
00:04:26,145 --> 00:04:27,321
Sounds like nothing.

84
00:04:27,468 --> 00:04:30,930
But light covers about
seven miles in that time.

85
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:36,220
For a system that turns time into
distance, that's seven miles of mistake.

86
00:04:36,390 --> 00:04:37,928
Added every single day.

87
00:04:40,048 --> 00:04:42,356
So what did the engineers do?

88
00:04:42,526 --> 00:04:44,449
They didn't argue with Einstein.

89
00:04:44,619 --> 00:04:45,388
They cheated.

90
00:04:45,558 --> 00:04:49,404
They set each satellite
clock to run a little slow.

91
00:04:49,575 --> 00:04:54,190
Its heartbeat should be ten point
two three million ticks a second.

92
00:04:54,360 --> 00:05:00,892
On the satellite, it's set to ten point
two two nine nine nine nine nine nine five

93
00:05:00,932 --> 00:05:01,788
four three.

94
00:05:01,958 --> 00:05:05,804
Slower by less than half
a part in a billion.

95
00:05:05,974 --> 00:05:09,171
On the ground, that clock would be wrong.

96
00:05:09,341 --> 00:05:15,350
In orbit, once Einstein is done with
it, it ticks at just the right rate.

97
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:21,305
And the team on the ground still checks
every clock and sends small corrections up

98
00:05:21,345 --> 00:05:22,058
every day.

99
00:05:24,178 --> 00:05:25,716
Did they trust Einstein?

100
00:05:25,887 --> 00:05:26,769
Not completely.

101
00:05:26,826 --> 00:05:31,561
In 1977, they launched a test satellite
with that correction switched off.

102
00:05:31,732 --> 00:05:32,886
Just in case.

103
00:05:33,055 --> 00:05:37,670
For about three weeks they
watched its clock run on its own.

104
00:05:37,841 --> 00:05:42,456
It ran fast by about four hundred
forty-three parts in a trillion.

105
00:05:42,626 --> 00:05:45,264
Einstein's prediction: about
four hundred forty-five.

106
00:05:45,304 --> 00:05:46,842
Within half a percent.

107
00:05:47,012 --> 00:05:49,320
Then they switched the correction on.

108
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:53,363
Want to see it yourself?

109
00:05:53,533 --> 00:05:59,939
Go outside, open a GPS status app on an
Android phone, and look at the satellites

110
00:05:59,979 --> 00:06:01,465
it hears right now.

111
00:06:01,635 --> 00:06:03,558
Find the ones marked GPS.

112
00:06:03,728 --> 00:06:08,728
Every one of them is carrying clocks
that were set slow on purpose.

113
00:06:10,848 --> 00:06:17,515
So next time the blue dot finds you, two
of Einstein's ideas just did the math for

114
00:06:17,555 --> 00:06:17,891
you.

115
00:06:18,062 --> 00:06:19,216
Speed slows time.

116
00:06:19,385 --> 00:06:20,539
Gravity slows time.

117
00:06:20,709 --> 00:06:24,171
And your phone needs both
to find your coffee.

118
00:06:24,341 --> 00:06:26,649
Now go check on the cat.

119
00:06:26,818 --> 00:06:29,510
It's been lying flat this whole time.

120
00:06:29,681 --> 00:06:30,835
Aging very evenly.

